commentBody:
@Vaer

But the problem is that you can’t know, up front, whether the overlays on a puzzle like today’s will be an integral part of an interesting, gimmicky theme (as I initially assumed would be the case today, given the day of week and byline) or training wheels that end up being a net negative (as the commas in today’s puzzle turned out to be).

If there was an option to toggle training-wheel overlays, I’d definitely keep that switched off…

@Marilyn

It wasn’t always like this. If you check out the comments to puzzles pre-2020 or so, it’s mostly people analyzing the puzzle, discussing the wordplay, etc. Sometime during the pandemic, the editors decided to make the puzzles much more straightforward and easy, presumably to attract new subscribers. 

Previously, the NYT crossword was generally regarded as the most creative and challenging one around. This led to the friction we see today, where some people pine for the quality of the old days, while others just want to get their gold star.

